 The BFD 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Bending Spoons yesterday secured a $330 million investment from existing shareholder Berkshire Hathaway .

 Why it's the BFD: The Italian software conglomerate, known for buying legacy brands, now has more dry powder for M&amp;A.

 Its shares also jumped more than 41% on the news, hitting an all-time high. Catch up quick: Berkshire Hathaway, now led by Greg Abel, first backed Bending Spoons in July, investing $600 million at IPO. Its stake is now around 4 percent.

 The bottom line: Hard to see software-averse Warren Buffett having done this deal.

 
 
